From: Christopher Owen <3221756+christopherowen@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 16 Oct 2026 14:01:27 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 145/274] DeepSeek-V4.1 Engram: disk shards on whole hash
 columns, rows joined by an all-gather

A layer's table is its 24 hash columns' prime-sized ranges back to back,
each just above the Engram vocabulary. Disk shards split the rows equally,
so a rank's shard cut a column (by about a thousand of its 16 million
rows on TP3 and TP4) and the ranks' gathered rows had to be summed: an
all-reduce of [T, 6144] per Engram layer, 26-30 ms of a TP3 16K prefill on
the rank that waits for no one.

When the TP size divides the 24 columns, a disk shard is now whole
columns (rows from offsets[24r/N] to offsets[24(r+1)/N], sizes within
0.002% of an equal split), so a rank's gathered rows are one contiguous
band of 24/N * 256 values, and an all-gather of the bands (half the bytes,
one phase) gives every rank the full rows. Adding zero rows no longer
turns a -0.0 into +0.0, as the reference never sums them. Resident tables
keep equal shards and the all-reduce.

Test: column shards tile the table at column offsets and their bands tile
the row in rank order (TP 1, 2, 3, 4, 6; none for 5).

